  IF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.viva_model_answers WHERE id = '30d1a2ce-2b0d-4ca3-bdb4-ccb52983544f' AND model_answer IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Local Anaesthetic Systemic Toxicity (LAST) is a life-threatening adverse event resulting from excessive plasma concentrations of local anaesthetics, typically following inadvertent intravascular injection or rapid absorption from highly vascular sites. The mechanism involves the blockade of voltage-gated sodium channels in cardiac and neural membranes, leading to central nervous system (CNS) and cardiovascular system (CVS) collapse.

Clinically, LAST may present classically with prodromal neurological symptoms (metallic taste, perioral tingling, tinnitus) followed by seizures or coma, or rapidly with sudden cardiovascular instability. CVS toxicity is characterised by conduction delay, re-entrant arrhythmias, and profound myocardial depression. Bupivacaine is particularly cardiotoxic due to its high lipid solubility and slow dissociation from channels.

Seizures and cardiovascular collapse can occur together, or cardiovascular signs can be the first presentation.

Stop the local anaesthetic and call for help and the lipid rescue pack. Give oxygen and support ventilation. Treat seizures with a benzodiazepine. In circulatory arrest start CPR, use IV adrenaline doses of 1 microgram/kg or less and avoid vasopressin. Consider cardiopulmonary bypass for refractory arrest. With a circulation, treat haemodynamic instability and consider lipid rescue according to severity.

UK Association of Anaesthetists QRH 3-10. For severe LAST, the initial IV dose of 20% lipid emulsion is 1.5 mL/kg, given over 2–3 minutes. Start at 15 mL/kg/hour. If circulation remains unstable or deteriorates, repeat the original bolus at 5 and 10 minutes as indicated. After 5 minutes the infusion may be increased to 30 mL/kg/hour for persistent or recurrent instability. Count all boluses and infusion towards the 12 mL/kg cumulative maximum.

Source for emergency management: https://anaesthetists.org/Portals/0/PDFs/QRH/QRH_3-10_Local_anaesthetic_toxicity_v2_June%202023.pdf?ver=2023-06-23-141010-760'
      AND high_yield_points IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '["Stop injecting and call for help/LAST kit","Secure airway with 100% oxygen","Use a benzodiazepine first for seizures.","UK QRH: 20% lipid, 1.5 mL/kg over 2–3 minutes, then 15 mL/kg/hour.","Avoid Vasopressin and Calcium Channel Blockers"]'::jsonb) THEN NULL;
  ELSE
    UPDATE public.viva_model_answers SET model_answer = 'Local Anaesthetic Systemic Toxicity (LAST) is a life-threatening adverse event resulting from excessive plasma concentrations of local anaesthetics, typically following inadvertent intravascular injection or rapid absorption from highly vascular sites. The mechanism involves the blockade of voltage-gated sodium channels in cardiac and neural membranes, leading to central nervous system (CNS) and cardiovascular system (CVS) collapse.

Clinically, LAST may present classically with prodromal neurological symptoms (metallic taste, perioral tingling, tinnitus) followed by seizures or coma, or rapidly with sudden cardiovascular instability. CVS toxicity is characterised by conduction delay, re-entrant arrhythmias, and profound myocardial depression. Bupivacaine is particularly cardiotoxic due to its high lipid solubility and slow dissociation from channels.

Seizures and cardiovascular collapse can occur together, or cardiovascular signs can be the first presentation.

Stop the local anaesthetic and call for help and the lipid rescue pack. Give oxygen and support ventilation. Treat seizures with a benzodiazepine. In circulatory arrest start CPR, use IV adrenaline doses of 1 microgram/kg or less and avoid vasopressin. Consider cardiopulmonary bypass for refractory arrest. With a circulation, treat haemodynamic instability and consider lipid rescue according to severity.

UK Association of Anaesthetists QRH 3-10. For severe LAST, the initial IV dose of 20% lipid emulsion is 1.5 mL/kg, given over 2–3 minutes. Start at 15 mL/kg/hour. If circulation remains unstable or deteriorates, repeat the original bolus at 5 and 10 minutes as indicated. After 5 minutes the infusion may be increased to 30 mL/kg/hour for persistent or recurrent instability. Count all boluses and infusion towards the 12 mL/kg cumulative maximum.

Source for emergency management: https://anaesthetists.org/Portals/0/PDFs/QRH/QRH_3-10_Local_anaesthetic_toxicity_v2_June%202023.pdf?ver=2023-06-23-141010-760',
      high_yield_points = '["Stop injecting and call for help/LAST kit","Secure airway with 100% oxygen","Use a benzodiazepine first for seizures.","UK QRH: 20% lipid, 1.5 mL/kg over 2–3 minutes, then 15 mL/kg/hour.","Avoid Vasopressin and Calcium Channel Blockers"]'::jsonb, updated_at=now()
    WHERE id = '30d1a2ce-2b0d-4ca3-bdb4-ccb52983544f' 
      AND model_answer IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Local Anaesthetic Systemic Toxicity (LAST) is a life-threatening adverse event resulting from excessive plasma concentrations of local anaesthetics, typically following inadvertent intravascular injection or rapid absorption from highly vascular sites. The mechanism involves the blockade of voltage-gated sodium channels in cardiac and neural membranes, leading to central nervous system (CNS) and cardiovascular system (CVS) collapse.

Clinically, LAST may present classically with prodromal neurological symptoms (metallic taste, perioral tingling, tinnitus) followed by seizures or coma, or rapidly with sudden cardiovascular instability. CVS toxicity is characterised by conduction delay, re-entrant arrhythmias, and profound myocardial depression. Bupivacaine is particularly cardiotoxic due to its high lipid solubility and slow dissociation from channels.

Management must follow the Association of Anaesthetists (AAGBI) guidelines. Immediate steps include stopping the drug, securing the airway with 100% oxygen, and controlling seizures. The definitive treatment is Intralipid 20% therapy. For a 70kg patient, this involves a 1.5ml/kg (approx 100ml) bolus over one minute, followed by an infusion at 0.25 ml/kg/min (15 ml/kg/hr). Boluses can be repeated twice at 5-minute intervals if stability is not achieved. Basic and Advanced Life Support should continue, noting that resuscitation may be prolonged (over 60 minutes). Specific pitfalls include avoiding vasopressin, using reduced adrenaline doses (<1mcg/kg), and ensuring the patient is not transferred for bypass prematurely as lipids often resolve the block.'
      AND high_yield_points IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '["Stop injecting and call for help/LAST kit","Secure airway with 100% oxygen","Manage seizures with Benzodiazepines/Propofol","Intralipid 20% bolus 1.5ml/kg followed by infusion","Avoid Vasopressin and Calcium Channel Blockers"]'::jsonb;
    IF NOT FOUND THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Unexpected content for viva_model_answers 30d1a2ce-2b0d-4ca3-bdb4-ccb52983544f'; END IF;
  END IF;
  IF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.viva_model_answers WHERE id = '319250a1-999a-4f0d-b013-937d8928f725' AND model_answer IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Local anaesthetics (LAs) are reversible sodium channel blockers that prevent the generation and conduction of nerve impulses. Structurally, they consist of a lipophilic aromatic ring and a hydrophilic amine group linked by an ester or amide bond. Their mechanism of action involves diffusing across the lipid bilayer in unionized form, becoming re-ionized intracellularly, and binding to the alpha-subunit of voltage-gated sodium channels from the internal aspect, primarily during the inactivated state.

The clinical profile of an LA is dictated by its physicochemical properties. Potency is directly proportional to lipid solubility (e.g., Bupivacaine vs Lidocaine). The speed of onset is determined by the pKa; LAs with a pKa closer to physiological pH (7.4) have a higher unionized fraction to cross the cell membrane. Duration of action is primarily determined by protein binding, specifically to alpha-1-acid glycoprotein.

Seizures and cardiovascular collapse can occur together, or cardiovascular signs can be the first presentation.

Stop the local anaesthetic and call for help and the lipid rescue pack. Give oxygen and support ventilation. Treat seizures with a benzodiazepine. In circulatory arrest start CPR, use IV adrenaline doses of 1 microgram/kg or less and avoid vasopressin. Consider cardiopulmonary bypass for refractory arrest. With a circulation, treat haemodynamic instability and consider lipid rescue according to severity.

UK Association of Anaesthetists QRH 3-10. For severe LAST, the initial IV dose of 20% lipid emulsion is 1.5 mL/kg, given over 2–3 minutes. Start at 15 mL/kg/hour. If circulation remains unstable or deteriorates, repeat the original bolus at 5 and 10 minutes as indicated. After 5 minutes the infusion may be increased to 30 mL/kg/hour for persistent or recurrent instability. Count all boluses and infusion towards the 12 mL/kg cumulative maximum.

Source for emergency management: https://anaesthetists.org/Portals/0/PDFs/QRH/QRH_3-10_Local_anaesthetic_toxicity_v2_June%202023.pdf?ver=2023-06-23-141010-760'
      AND high_yield_points IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '["Na+ channel blockade (inner pore) in the inactivated state.","pKa determines onset: closer to physiological pH means more unionized fraction.","Protein binding (AAG) determines duration of action.","Lipid solubility determines potency.","UK QRH: 20% lipid, 1.5 mL/kg over 2–3 minutes, then 15 mL/kg/hour."]'::jsonb) THEN NULL;
  ELSE
    UPDATE public.viva_model_answers SET model_answer = 'Local anaesthetics (LAs) are reversible sodium channel blockers that prevent the generation and conduction of nerve impulses. Structurally, they consist of a lipophilic aromatic ring and a hydrophilic amine group linked by an ester or amide bond. Their mechanism of action involves diffusing across the lipid bilayer in unionized form, becoming re-ionized intracellularly, and binding to the alpha-subunit of voltage-gated sodium channels from the internal aspect, primarily during the inactivated state.

The clinical profile of an LA is dictated by its physicochemical properties. Potency is directly proportional to lipid solubility (e.g., Bupivacaine vs Lidocaine). The speed of onset is determined by the pKa; LAs with a pKa closer to physiological pH (7.4) have a higher unionized fraction to cross the cell membrane. Duration of action is primarily determined by protein binding, specifically to alpha-1-acid glycoprotein.

