UK Association of Anaesthetists QRH 3-10. For severe LAST, the initial IV dose of 20% lipid emulsion is 1.5 mL/kg, given over 2–3 minutes. Start at 15 mL/kg/hour. If circulation remains unstable or deteriorates, repeat the original bolus at 5 and 10 minutes as indicated. After 5 minutes the infusion may be increased to 30 mL/kg/hour for persistent or recurrent instability. Count all boluses and infusion towards the 12 mL/kg cumulative maximum.

Source for emergency management: https://anaesthetists.org/Portals/0/PDFs/QRH/QRH_3-10_Local_anaesthetic_toxicity_v2_June%202023.pdf?ver=2023-06-23-141010-760',
      high_yield_points = '["Na+ channel blockade (inner pore) in the inactivated state.","pKa determines onset: closer to physiological pH means more unionized fraction.","Protein binding (AAG) determines duration of action.","Lipid solubility determines potency.","UK QRH: 20% lipid, 1.5 mL/kg over 2–3 minutes, then 15 mL/kg/hour."]'::jsonb, updated_at=now()
    WHERE id = '319250a1-999a-4f0d-b013-937d8928f725' 
      AND model_answer IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Local anaesthetics (LAs) are reversible sodium channel blockers that prevent the generation and conduction of nerve impulses. Structurally, they consist of a lipophilic aromatic ring and a hydrophilic amine group linked by an ester or amide bond. Their mechanism of action involves diffusing across the lipid bilayer in unionized form, becoming re-ionized intracellularly, and binding to the alpha-subunit of voltage-gated sodium channels from the internal aspect, primarily during the inactivated state.

The clinical profile of an LA is dictated by its physicochemical properties. Potency is directly proportional to lipid solubility (e.g., Bupivacaine vs Lidocaine). The speed of onset is determined by the pKa; LAs with a pKa closer to physiological pH (7.4) have a higher unionized fraction to cross the cell membrane. Duration of action is primarily determined by protein binding, specifically to alpha-1-acid glycoprotein.

Local Anaesthetic Systemic Toxicity (LAST) is a critical complication. It manifests as a continuum of CNS symptoms (tinnitus, perioral tingling, seizures, coma) followed by cardiovascular collapse (arrhythmias, profound hypotension). Management requires immediate cessation of the drug, 100% oxygen, and seizure control. If cardiovascular instability occurs, the ''Lipid Rescue'' protocol is initiated: 20% Intralipid is given as a 1.5 mL/kg bolus over 1 minute, followed by an infusion at 15 mL/kg/h for all patients (approximately 1000 mL/h in an adult over 70 kg) (Association of Anaesthetists 2023). CPR must be continued throughout as LAs can be slowly sequestered by the ''lipid sink''. Maximum doses in a 70kg adult are approximately 200mg for Lidocaine (3mg/kg) and 150mg for Bupivacaine (2mg/kg).'
      AND high_yield_points IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '["Na+ channel blockade (inner pore) in the inactivated state.","pKa determines onset: closer to physiological pH means more unionized fraction.","Protein binding (AAG) determines duration of action.","Lipid solubility determines potency.","Intralipid 20% dosing: 1.5 mL/kg bolus, then 15 mL/kg/hr infusion."]'::jsonb;
    IF NOT FOUND THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Unexpected content for viva_model_answers 319250a1-999a-4f0d-b013-937d8928f725'; END IF;
