  IF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.viva_model_answers WHERE id = 'e800a791-06e5-44e5-aa0d-ed85215099d9' AND model_answer IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Local Anaesthetic Systemic Toxicity (LAST) is a life-threatening emergency resulting from high plasma concentrations of local anaesthetic, either via accidental intravascular injection or rapid absorption from highly vascular sites (e.g., intercostal blocks). 

The mechanism involves the blockade of voltage-gated sodium channels, but at toxic levels, it also affects potassium and calcium channels and disrupts mitochondrial metabolism. The clinical presentation is classically divided into CNS and Cardiovascular systems. CNS symptoms usually precede cardiac signs and include circumoral tingling, metallic taste, tinnitus, and slurred speech, progressing to grand mal seizures and coma. Cardiovascular toxicity is more ominous, characterized by myocardial depression, conduction delay (widened QRS and prolonged PR), re-entrant arrhythmias, and ultimately refractory asystole. Potent long-acting amides like bupivacaine are particularly cardiotoxic due to their slow dissociation from sodium channels (''fast-in, slow-out'' kinetics).

Seizures and cardiovascular collapse can occur together, or cardiovascular signs can be the first presentation.

Stop the local anaesthetic and call for help and the lipid rescue pack. Give oxygen and support ventilation. Treat seizures with a benzodiazepine. In circulatory arrest start CPR, use IV adrenaline doses of 1 microgram/kg or less and avoid vasopressin. Consider cardiopulmonary bypass for refractory arrest. With a circulation, treat haemodynamic instability and consider lipid rescue according to severity.

