  IF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.viva_model_answers WHERE id = 'dc8dda82-d8cf-4d8b-948f-6fb423cb48ea' AND model_answer IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Malignant hyperthermia (MH) is a rare, life-threatening pharmacogenetic hypermetabolic state of skeletal muscle triggered by volatile anaesthetics or succinylcholine. Pathophysiologically, it involves an autosomal dominant mutation in the ryanodine receptor (RYR1), leading to uncontrolled calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, resulting in sustained muscle contraction, excessive CO2 production, and heat.

Initial clinical signs are often non-specific but include an unexplained rise in end-tidal CO2 (often the earliest sign), tachycardia, and masseter muscle rigidity. Late signs involve hyperthermia, metabolic acidosis, hyperkalaemia, and eventually multi-organ failure.

Call for help immediately, stop triggering agents and use high-flow 100% oxygen with increased minute ventilation. Remove the vaporiser. If available, place activated charcoal filters on both limbs. A machine or circuit change must not delay treatment. Continue trigger-free anaesthesia if required.

EMHG crisis protocol. Give IV dantrolene promptly, 2–2.5 mg/kg using actual body weight, capped at 300 mg per dose. Repeat at 10-minute intervals, or as soon as feasible if administration itself takes longer. Assess the fall in core temperature and whether PaCO₂ falls below 6 kPa with normal minute ventilation. If more than 10 mg/kg in total is needed, reconsider other diagnoses while treating ongoing signs.

Follow the local MH protocol for cooling, hyperkalaemia, rhabdomyolysis and organ support. Monitor for recurrence for at least 24 hours and refer to an MH investigation centre. For relatives or patients with suspected susceptibility, a planned trigger-free technique can include regional or general anaesthesia.

Sources: https://www.emhg.org/recommendations-1/2024/11/4/recognising-and-managing-a-malignant-hyperthermia-crisis-v2024 and https://www.emhg.org/recommendations-1/2018/2/27/perioperative-management-of-mh-suspected-or-susceptible-patients'
