      AND model_answer IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Malignant hyperthermia (MH) is a rare, life-threatening pharmacogenetic hypermetabolic state of skeletal muscle triggered by volatile anaesthetics or succinylcholine. Pathophysiologically, it involves an autosomal dominant mutation in the ryanodine receptor (RYR1), leading to uncontrolled calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, resulting in sustained muscle contraction, excessive CO2 production, and heat.

Initial clinical signs are often non-specific but include an unexplained rise in end-tidal CO2 (often the earliest sign), tachycardia, and masseter muscle rigidity. Late signs involve hyperthermia, metabolic acidosis, hyperkalaemia, and eventually multi-organ failure.

Management must be immediate using the NAP6 and AAGBI-endorsed protocols. First, stop the trigger, call for help, and switch to a TIVA technique using a clean circuit/charcoal filters. The specific treatment is Dantrolene, an intracellular muscle relaxant that binds to the RYR1 receptor, inhibiting uncontrolled calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. The dose is 2.5 mg/kg IV bolus, repeated every 5-10 minutes until the reaction is controlled (max dose usually cited as 10 mg/kg). Supportive care includes treating hyperkalaemia with insulin/dextrose, active cooling (surface and cold IV fluids), and ensuring high urine output (>2 ml/kg/hr) to avoid renal failure from rhabdomyolysis. Post-crisis, the patient requires ICU monitoring for 24 hours and referral to the national MH unit in Leeds for muscle biopsy (caffeine-halothane contracture test) or genetic screening.'
